  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{\u2033}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =6.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4-{e}^{6x}$ is not a solution.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
